WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.680
I talk a lot about the power of
taking personal responsibility

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.160
for your own health,
for your family’s health.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.680
But there are forces at play when it
comes to influencing what we eat.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:11.865
Check it out.

00:00:11.865 --> 00:00:18.348
"The Role of Marketing and Food Advertisements
in the Obesity Epidemic"

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.640
In the 1970s the U.S. government
went from just subsidizing some

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.740
of the worst foods to paying
companies to make more of them.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.360
In the 1970’s the farm bills
reversed longstanding policies

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.480
aimed at limiting production
to protect prices and instead

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.200
started giving payouts in
proportion to output.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.960
Extra calories started pouring
into the food supply.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:45.840
Then Jack Welch gave a speech.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.440
In 1981, the CEO of General Electric

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:51.440
effectively launched the
“shareholder value movement,”

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.800
reorienting the primary
goal of corporations

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.440
towards maximizing short-term
returns for investors.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.640
This placed extraordinary pressures
on food companies from Wall Street

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:08.520
to post increasing profit growth every
quarter to boost their share price.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.200
There was already a glut
of calories on the market,

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.200
and now they had to sell even more.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:18.720
This places food and beverage
CEOs in an impossible bind.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.320
It’s not like they’re rubbing
their sticky hands together

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:23.640
at the thought of luring
more Hansels and Gretels

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.760
to their doom in their houses of candy.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.840
Food giants couldn’t do
the right thing if they wanted.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.960
They are beholden to investors.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.800
If they stopped marketing to kids,

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.440
or tried to sell healthier food, or anything

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:38.760
that could jeopardize their
quarterly profit growth,

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.520
Wall Street would demand a
change in management.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.840
Healthy eating is bad for business.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.840
It’s not some grand conspiracy;
it’s not even anyone’s fault, really.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:54.440
It’s just how the system works.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.800
Given the constant demands for
corporate growth and rapid returns

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:00.360
in an already oversaturated marketplace,

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.480
the food industry needed
to get people to eat more.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:09.920
Like the tobacco industry before
them, they turned to the ad men.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.880
The food industry spends about
$10 billion a year on advertising

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.080
and around another $20 billion
on other forms of marketing

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:20.000
such as trade shows, incentives,
consumer promotions,

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.560
and supermarket “slotting fees."

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.240
Food and beverage companies purchase
shelf space from supermarkets

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:29.000
to prominently display their
most profitable products.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.560
They pay supermarkets.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.040
The practice is also evidently
known as “cliffing,”

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.000
because companies are forced to
bid against each other for eye level

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.240
shelf placement with the loser
being pushed “over the cliff.”

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:46.960
With slotting fees up to $20,000
per item, per retailer, per city,

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:50.120
you can imagine what kind of
products get the special treatment.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.200
Hint: It ain’t broccoli.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.760
To get a sense of what kind of
products merit prime shelf real estate,

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.680
look no further than the checkout aisle.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.840
“Merchandising the power categories
on every lane is critical,”

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.920
reads a trade publication
on the “best practices

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.080
for superior checkout merchandising.”

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:15.040
They were referring to
candy bars and beverages.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.040
Evidently even a 1 percent
power category boost in sales

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:22.760
could earn a store
an extra $15,000 a year.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:27.040
It’s not that they necessarily don’t
care about their customers’ health;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:31.640
publicly traded companies, like most of
the leading grocery store chains,

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.040
are said to have a fiduciary duty

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:38.960
to increase profits above
other considerations.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:42.040
Tens of millions of dollars
are spent annually

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:45.800
advertising a single brand of candy bar.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.160
McDonald’s alone may
spend billions a year.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.800
The food industry now spends
more money on advertising

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.240
than any other sector of the economy.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.400
Reagan-era deregulation removed
the limits placed on marketing

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.920
food products on television to children.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.240
Now the average child may see
more than 10,000 food ads a year,

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.200
and that’s on top of the marketing
online, in print, at school,

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:11.640
on their phones, at the movies,
and everywhere in between.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:17.320
And nearly all of it for products
detrimental to their health.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.360
Besides the massive early
exposure and ubiquity,

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:24.920
food marketing has become
highly sophisticated.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:27.920
With the help of child
psychologists, companies learn

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:31.840
how to best influence children
to manipulate their parents.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.720
Packaging is designed to best
attract a child’s attention,

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.760
and then placed at their
eye level in the store.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.880
You know those mirrored bubbles
in the ceilings of supermarkets?

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:43.920
They’re not just for shoplifters.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.840
Closed-circuit cameras
and GPS-like devices

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:51.360
on shopping carts are used to
strategize how best to guide shoppers

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.480
towards their most profitable products.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.800
Behavioral psychology is widely
applied to increase impulse buying.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.160
Eye movement tracking
technologies are utilized.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:06.200
The unprecedented rise in the
power, scope, and sophistication

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.960
of food marketing starting around 1980,

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:13.840
that aligns well with the blastoff
slope of the obesity epidemic.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:17.760
Some of the techniques, such as product
placement, in-school advertising,

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:21.440
and event sponsorships skyrocketed
from essentially zero

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:24.440
to multibillion-dollar
industries since the 80s.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:27.200
This led one economist
to conclude that

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:31.880
“the most compelling single interpretation
of the admittedly incomplete data

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:37.640
we have is that the large increase
in obesity is due to marketing.”

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:41.040
Yes, innovations in manufacturing
and political maneuvering led to a

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:47.120
food supply bursting at the seams with
close to 4,000 calories a day for us all,

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.080
but it’s the advances in
marketing manipulations

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:56.804
that are used to try to peddle
that surplus into our mouths.

00:05:58.353 --> 00:06:00.560
The opening words of the
Institute of Medicine’s report

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:04.040
on the potential threat
posed by food ads were:

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:06.360
“Marketing works.”

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.480
Yeah, there's a large
number of well-conducted

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:12.000
randomized studies I could go through
showing advertising exposure

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.120
and how other marketing methods can
change your eating behavior

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:18.840
and get you to eat more, but
what do you need to know beyond

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:23.640
the fact that the industry spends tens
of billions of dollars a year on it.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:26.920
To get people to drink their
brown sugar water, do you think

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:30.640
Coca-Cola would spend a penny more
than they thought they had to?

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:33.880
It’s like when my medical
colleagues accept “drug lunches”

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:37.040
from pharmaceutical representatives
and take offense

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:40.440
that I would suggest it might
affect their prescribing practices.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:44.320
Do they really think drug
companies are just in the business

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:47.160
of giving away free money for nothing?

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.920
They wouldn’t do it if it didn’t work.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:54.200
To give you a sense of marketing’s
insidious nature, though,

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.600
let me share an interesting
piece of research

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.400
published in the world’s
leading scientific journal:

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:05.000
“In-store music affects product
choice” documented an experiment

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.960
in which either French accordion
or German Bierkeller music

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.160
was played on alternate days in
the wine section of a grocery store.

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:15.720
On the days the French music
was playing in the background

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:18.680
people were three times more
likely to buy French wine,

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:22.240
and on German music days
shoppers were about three times

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.240
likely more to buy German wine.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:29.000
Despite the dramatic effect—
not just a few percent difference

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:33.440
but a complete three-fold reversal—
when approached afterwards,

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:39.560
the vast majority of shoppers denied the
music had any influence on their choice.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:42.320
We all like to think we
make important life decisions

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:45.080
like what to eat consciously
and rationally.

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:47.880
However, if that were
the case, we wouldn’t be

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:50.880
in the midst of an obesity epidemic.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:55.440
Most of our day-to-day behavior
does not appear to be dictated

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:58.280
by careful, considered deliberations.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:01.000
Rather, we tend to make more automatic,

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:05.880
impulsive decisions triggered by
unconscious cues and habitual patterns,

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:09.680
especially when we’re
tired, stressed, or preoccupied.

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:12.400
The unconscious part of
our brain is thought to guide

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:15.960
human behaviors as much
as 95 percent of the time.

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:21.200
This is the arena where marketing
manipulations do their dirty work.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:24.280
The part of our brain that
governs conscious awareness

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:28.160
may only be able to process about
50 bits of information per second,

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:31.040
which is roughly equivalent
to a short tweet.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:33.640
Our entire cognitive
capacity, on the other hand,

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:38.000
is estimated to process in excess
of 10 million bits per second.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.560
Because we’re only able to
purposefully process a limited amount

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:44.840
of information at a time,
if we’re distracted or otherwise

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:49.120
unable to concentrate, our decisions
can become even more impulsive.

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:52.280
An elegant illustration of
this “cognitive overload” effect

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:58.760
was provided from an experiment
involving fruit salad and chocolate cake.

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:03.080
Before calls could be made at a touch
of a button or the sound of your voice,

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:06.560
the seven-digit span of
phone numbers was based in part

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:11.160
on the longest sequence most
people can recall on the fly.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:14.200
We only seem able to hold
about seven chunks of information

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:17.640
(plus or minus two) in our
immediate short-term memory.

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:21.520
OK, so here’s the setup:
randomize people to memorize

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:24.640
either a seven-digit
number or a two-digit number

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:28.120
to be recalled in another
room down the hall.

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:33.120
On the way, offer them the choice of a
fruit salad or a piece of chocolate cake

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:35.600
Memorizing a two-digit number is easy

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:38.720
and presumably takes few
cognitive resources.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.440
Under the two-digit condition,
most chose the fruit salad.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:46.480
Faced with the same decision,
most of those trying to keep

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:50.160
the seven digits in their
heads just went for the cake.

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:54.560
This can play out in the real world by
potentiating the effect of advertising.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:57.520
Have people watch a TV show with
commercials for unhealthy snacks

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:00.840
and, no surprise, they eat
more unhealthy snacks,

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:03.360
(compared to those exposed
to non-food ads).

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:05.680
Or maybe that is a surprise.

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:10.400
We all like to think that we’re in
control and not so easily manipulatable.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:15.800
The kicker, though, is that we
may even be more susceptible

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:17.480
the less we pay attention.

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:21.280
Randomize people to the same two-digit
or seven-digit memorization task

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:25.640
during the show, and the snack
attack effect was magnified

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:27.880
among those who
were more preoccupied.

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:31.520
How many of us have the
TV on in the background or

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:33.840
multi-task during commercial breaks?

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:37.720
This research suggests that it may
make us even more impressionable

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:41.400
to the subversion of our better judgement.

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:43.560
There’s an irony in all this.

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:46.920
Calls for restrictions on
marketing are often resisted

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:49.280
by invoking the banner of freedom.

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:52.400
What does that even mean in this
context, when research shows how

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:57.920
easily our free choices can be
influenced without our conscious control.

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:00.640
A senior policy researcher at
the RAND Corporation even went

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:04.080
as far as to suggest that given
the dire health consequences

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:08.600
of our unhealthy eating habits,
insidious marketing manipulations

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:11.720
“should be considered in
the same light as the invisible

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:14.720
carcinogens and toxins
in the air and water

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:20.353
that can poison us without our awareness.”

